criteria.notes:
The wire schema is byte-for-byte the spec's (deep-frozen), and validateExtraction enforces exact keys, enums, deadline patterns, budget decimals by string arithmetic and null <=> none.
- Its optional text context also checks that a number occurs in the quote. That is the check d3 does not use: TR-D-1.
- The gate works in integer micro-dollars:
  - the rate count and insert run in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE;
  - concurrency, global budget and customer share are checked in one transaction before the call;
  - spend + cap = budget passes;
  - unreadable usage is charged at the cap.
- My budget-boundary mutant (>=) is killed by d3's API tests too, not only by d1's unit tests.
- selectProvider returns the fake whenever RS_TEST_OFFLINE=1.
- Rephrase is built and unit-tested but not wired (FU-25, dec-mv330t7a-017a2211). I agree: an inert switch is not a feature, and the decision has e1 and g2 remove or document it.

CLI adapter:
- no shell, its own process group;
- an allow-listed environment rebuilt from scratch, with no RS_* name and no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY;
- a temp cwd removed before settling, an 8 MiB stdout cap, SIGKILL of the group on timeout or abort, one attempt, at most 2 concurrent calls.
All 13 pinned flags exist in the real CLI 2.1.296 (ev:ev-mv335hqq-0176e557, see TR-D-5).

Anthropic adapter:
- the base URL is allow-listed, redirect is 'error', and the response is capped at 1 MiB;
- one retry on 5xx, only inside the deadline;
- 429/529 map to PROVIDER_BUSY; refusal and max_tokens to PROVIDER_BAD_OUTPUT.

EXT-1 corpus: 16 text cases with 5 AR/EN pairs, 3 injection cases, 6 PNGs (3 clear, 2 ambiguous, 1 injection), and the smoke case. I looked at i05 (really unreadable) and i06 (legible injection text). The spec asks for labels/*.html; the delivered labels/*.txt are delta 9 in TR-D-4.

The metric thresholds equal the spec table, and the negative controls show that every metric can fail (TR-D-6).

The live runner refuses without RS_LIVE_MODEL=1 (exit 2, the plan's message) and stops before the ceiling. LIVE-M1 is not claimed.

FU-17, my own Chromium run at 360x800 after a successful extraction (not the degraded one d5 pins):
- English and Arabic both stack as display:block;
- page scrollWidth is 360, with no overflowing cell;
- every cell is 292 px wide and shows its column title through ::before;
- the Arabic wording renders dir=auto (for example "Your words: ٢٥٠ مل");
- no console, CSP or HTTP problems.
The screenshot is in my scratchpad, not the tree.

The degraded notice, the manual form with every field, the AI-addressed notice and the Simulated badge are covered by the d4 unit tests and the RS-03 browser journey.

The degraded form stays open after confirmation (dec-mv32c290-0182b807). I agree: it is the only way to correct hand-typed values, and the stale notice is hidden.

